WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Okay, so let's get stuck in to video
number nine, our next leading indicator,
so our fifth leading indicator, the
University of Michigan Consumer Sentiment
Index, or the UMCSI.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:34.880
So yes, we are going to be having a look
now at consumers and a survey in the
consumer sector of the economy.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:45.080
So, recap of leading indicators covered so
far. We've got our government bond markets
leading indicators, real interest rates
and the yield curve.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:52.960
We've got our corporate bond markets
leading indicators, our investment grade
with high yield or junk spreads over the
10-year.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:05.880
We've got our M2 money supply accessory.
We've got our stock market volatility, the
VIX, the S&P 500 VIX from the IPLT video
series, and we've got our surveys.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:15.760
And so far, we have covered the ISM
Manufacturing Report on Business, and
we've covered the ISM Non-Manufacturing
Report on Business.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:25.760
Now, we are going to add in the US
consumer by looking at the University of
Michigan Consumer Sentiment Index.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:39.640
So what exactly is the UMCSI? What is it?
Well, it's a monthly survey that gauges
the level of consumer sentiment in the US
economy.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:49.520
So, sentiment as to whether consumers as a
whole in the US economy are either bullish
or bearish on their future.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:02:05.520
So, if consumer sentiment is high, so
consumers in the US are bullish on their
future, consumers are likely to be
spending more in the future, causing the
economy to expand.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:11.400
So there's more consumption and
potentially less saving.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:27.520
If consumer sentiment is low, so bearish
on the future, so consumers in the US are
looking at their future, and they're
bearish on their consumer sentiment for
the future.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:35.400
So, what's going to happen? Consumers will
likely spend less in the future, causing
the economy to contract.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:41.400
So we get less consumption and people
tightening their belts and saving more.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:56.960
Now, the consumer, we calculate the size
of the consumer in the US in terms of the
amount of consumer spending that takes
place in the economy.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:02.840
And consumer spending makes up 67% of the
US economy.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.840
And if you recall, there's not many
entities that make up an economy.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.840
It's just businesses, consumers, and
government.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:17.840
And businesses and consumers are in the
private sector, and government is in the
public sector.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:27.720
So if we're looking at consumer spending
making up 67% of the economy, the
remainder is business and government
spending.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:34.720
So the consumer, US consumer spending
makes up the majority of spending of those
entities in the United States.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:55.600
So it obviously stands to reason that as
traders and portfolio managers, gauging
consumer sentiment in the United States is
extremely important as a leading indicator
to the future of the economy, so GDP
growth.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:13.480
Now, before we get into the details of the
UMCSI, it's probably prudent at this stage
to have a quick lesson on some economic
theory to understand the dynamics of
consumers.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:26.360
So what we're going to have a look at
first is something called the paradox of
thrift, which was popularized by the
famous economist John Maynard Keynes.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:42.320
And it's based on the notion, which we
outlined briefly on the previous slide,
whereby individuals seek to cut their
consumption during periods of economic
contraction and increase their savings.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:50.800
So tightening their belt overall, which in
turn will lead to further contraction.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:05:04.120
So think about this, when there's an
economic downturn, if all that consumers
are reading in the media are headlines
like, get ready for the recession, what's
their natural tendency going to be?

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:12.120
Their natural tendency is going to be to
cut consumption, to save and hoard cash
and resources.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:15.760
And what does that lead to in the future?

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:37.900
Well, it causes a steeper and faster
contraction in the economy, in GDP, so GDP
growth, because of the sudden fall in
consumer demand, which leads to a longer
and deeper period of contraction.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:48.100
So the paradox occurs because the only way
really for an economy to get itself out of
contraction is to obviously expand.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:53.020
And the only way to expand is to actually
spend.

00:05:53.820 --> 00:06:03.980
So the spending causes an increase in
aggregate demand, which generates price
inflation and then GDP growth.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:13.160
However, when individual consumers expect
worse economic conditions in the future,
their sentiment of the future is negative.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:21.280
And instead of doing what's required,
which is to spend in order to expand, what
do they do in reality?

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:26.760
They do the opposite and they save and
they cut consumer spending.

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:49.940
And collectively, if we take all consumers
in an economy, if they're all making this
decision at an individual level, and then
we look at all consumers in the economy,
by not spending collectively, consumers
are essentially causing their own economic
situation or living standards to be even
worse in the future.

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:58.120
So this is the paradox of thrift.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:05.160
So being thrifty in an economic downturn,
because all the consumers, it's in their
best interests for the economy to expand.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:10.360
However, their actions are the opposite of
what's good for them.

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:29.440
Now, if we think of the paradox of thrift
in reverse, well, it's a little
far-fetched to say that it's in the best
interests when an economy is expanding
that consumers would want.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:34.040
So it's in their best interests, they
would want an economy to contract.

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:35.600
So think about it.

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:45.820
If an economy is expanding for a long
period of time, why would consumers want
the economy to contract?

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:48.500
It's not in their best interest.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:08:03.400
So when we look at the paradox of thrift
in reverse, there's an issue here, because
as far as consumers and long-term
investors in the stock market are
concerned, let's ask ourselves this
question.

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:09.920
Is it bad to always be expanding and
benefiting from higher living standards?

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:11.460
Well, of course not.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:27.160
Basically, for living standards and your
own individual economic condition as a
consumer, contraction is bad and expansion
is good.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:51.580
So the behavior of consumers in times of
thrift, so during times of contraction, is
not consistent with consumer behavior in
times of expansion during, for lack of a
better word, during times of extravagance.

00:08:51.580 --> 00:09:14.680
So if we apply the same logic to an
economy that's expanding a lot for a long
period of time, if we wanted to make them
consistent, we would be stating that
consumers should be spending less and
saving more in order to better their own
living standards or economic situation.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:26.080
But this would be counterintuitive,
because if all consumers did this, it
would cause them to all have worse living
standards.

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:27.220
Why?

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:38.820
Because overall economic growth in the
economy would go down, and that's bad for
everybody, everybody's living standards
and economic situation individually.

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:51.240
So when consumer sentiment is high, and
we're in times of extravagance, it can be
argued that consumers are actually
behaving rationally.

00:09:51.360 --> 00:10:01.880
So they are spending more and more and
continually causing their own living
standards or economic situation to get
better and better.

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:12.200
And when consumer sentiment is low, it can
obviously be argued that consumers are
behaving irrationally.

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:19.480
So they are cautious, they are hoarding,
they are saving, and what ends up
happening?

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:27.560
They cause their own living standards or
economic situation to get worse and worse.

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:31.840
And what they should be doing is spending.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:46.340
So this is an interesting dynamic of
consumer behavior to understand as we lead
in to doing work on the University of
Michigan Consumer Sentiment Index.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:53.820
It's just good background information in
terms of economic understanding of how
consumers behave.

00:10:53.820 --> 00:11:11.200
We will read into our numbers in a lot
better understanding if we understand how
consumers behave in periods of thrift or
contraction and periods of expansion and
extravagance.

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:28.000
Now, something else to understand when
we're thinking about consumers, it's not a
consumer behavior or, if you like,
consumer psychology variable or factor to
consider.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:30.700
This is a capacity issue.

00:11:31.220 --> 00:11:38.180
We do have finite resources and consumers
have finite parameters.

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:43.660
One limiting factor is just the purchase
of products, okay?

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:56.580
A consumer can only have, at any one time,
so many cars, washing machines,
refrigerators, handheld devices, phones,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:11:57.580 --> 00:12:01.320
This is a finite constraint on consumers.

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:08.680
So when we think about that in terms of
extravagance, there is a cycle to this,
okay?

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:18.400
There is a cycle where consumers get
constrained and then there's nothing left
to consume.

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:22.740
Logically and in theory, that can be the
case.

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:36.860
Another limiting factor and probably much
more important from a trader's and
portfolio manager's perspective is that
consumers in their behaviors,

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:48.260
so consumer spending and savings rates,
are also a function of the money supply
and the price of money, so real interest
rates,

00:12:48.920 --> 00:13:00.040
both of which can be decreased and
increased by the Fed when they're
utilizing conventional and unconventional
means to hit their dual mandate.

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:09.160
So this is a real, everyday parameter
that's finite in the economy.

00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:15.780
Consumer spending has peaks and troughs
like business activity.

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:23.820
So when we looked at the ISM and the NMI,
they move in tandem and literally taking
together.

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:31.980
So when we take together consumer spending
and business activity, they make up the
business cycle.

00:13:32.580 --> 00:13:40.680
So if you recall from video four, when we
looked at real interest rates in the yield
curve, you may want to go back and have a
look at this slide.

00:13:41.260 --> 00:13:51.340
I've put it on the screen there for you to
remind you there's cycles to this based on
interest rates, okay, based on real rates
and the money supply.

00:13:52.540 --> 00:14:03.020
Money becomes cheap and money becomes
expensive and consumer spending responds
to this parameter and so do businesses.

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:08.980
Businesses, so businesses and consumers,
so businesses and consumers expand and
contract together.

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:21.360
Business investment and consumer spending
patterns or behaviors are to a large
extent a function of real rates and the
money supply.

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:37.140
So if you recall, just looking at the
screenshot of the slide from video four
there, when money becomes cheap,
governments, companies and individuals, so
consumers, borrow more of it.

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:41.020
We get higher growth prospects,
consumption.

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:46.540
So from consumers, we get higher
consumption and investment from
businesses.

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:56.000
How does that lead into the equity markets
where we get higher equity valuation and
earnings from companies.

00:14:56.180 --> 00:15:00.660
So we get higher corporate earnings and
higher equity market valuation.

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:07.160
And then we come to the end of the cycle
and then we come to the end of the cycle
and money becomes more expensive.

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:21.160
And what happens then, governments,
companies and individuals, or are less, we
get lower growth prospects or perhaps
contraction, we get less consumption and
investment, we get lower equity valuation
and earnings.

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:28.160
And then we start the cycle again, money
starts to become cheap.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:33.160
So this is how consumers fit in to the
overall picture.

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:37.160
Businesses and consumers expand and
contract together.

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:50.160
And quarterly and annual gross domestic
product numbers, so the GDP prints, lag
the S&P 500 price and earnings.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:16:01.160
Businesses and earnings and valuation,
which are a function of consumer spending
patterns and behaviours and business
investment.

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:19.160
So when we're considering consumers in the
economy, we not only have to understand
consumer behaviour and consumer
psychology, if you like, with the paradox
of thrift.

00:16:19.160 --> 00:16:24.160
So the opposite doesn't line up, doesn't
make logical sense.

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:39.160
We also have to consider how everything is
linked together and how consumers face a
finite set of variables or parameters.

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:42.160
So real interest rates and the money
supply.

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:49.160
So we don't just look at consumers in
absolute terms.

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:59.160
We have to consider consumer behaviour in
the context of everything else that is
happening in the economy.

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:08.160
Therefore, from a trader and portfolio
manager perspective, everything that's
happening with the rest of our leading
indicators,

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:14.160
especially what's happening in the money
markets and with businesses.

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:24.160
So both in the manufacturing and services,
so non-manufacturing sectors of the
economy.

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:29.160
Now, there are critics of the paradox of
thrift.

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:44.160
Quick reminder of what we've laid out so
far in terms of consumer behaviour, in
terms of rational behaviour, where there's
no paradox.

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:49.160
This is during periods of expansion, so
periods of extravagance.

00:17:49.160 --> 00:18:00.160
If consumer sentiment is high, so they are
bullish on the future, consumers will
spend more in the future, causing the
economy to expand more.

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:05.160
So in this period, what's the consumer
behaving like?

00:18:05.160 --> 00:18:11.160
There's more consumption, less savings, so
possibly not getting richer.

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:23.160
But during these periods of expansion,
they are associated with higher innovation
in the economy, more products and higher
living standards.

00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:29.160
So consumers are buying more products to
improve their living standards.

00:18:30.160 --> 00:18:32.160
What about the paradox of thrift?

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:34.160
So the irrational behaviour.

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:44.160
Well, if consumer sentiment is low, so
they're bearish on the future, consumers
spend less in the future.

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:47.160
This causes the economy to contract.

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:51.160
So less consumption and more savings.

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:56.160
So they're definitely not getting richer
during this time.

00:18:57.160 --> 00:18:59.160
There's less innovation.

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:07.160
They're consuming less products and their
living standards are staying the same or
perhaps getting worse.

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:18.160
Now, we looked on the previous slide at
the finite dynamics, the limiting factors
that affect consumer behaviour.

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:21.160
And how does this all linked together?

00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:33.160
Well, through the economic cycle,
supposedly, during times of extravagance,
interest rates will go higher.

00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:35.160
Real interest rates will go higher.

00:19:35.160 --> 00:19:49.160
And consumers are supposed to, in theory,
respond in their behaviour by increasing
their savings rate and decreasing their
consumption.

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:53.160
But in reality, that doesn't happen.

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:59.160
Periods of extravagance tend to last a lot
longer than expected.

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:05.160
And consumers tend to consume even more.

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:10.160
And when consumers do this, there's
actually no paradox.

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:20.160
This is actually rational behaviour
because continued economic growth is just
good for everybody.

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:24.160
It's better for everybody's living
standards.

00:20:24.160 --> 00:20:27.160
And what happens in periods of
contraction?

00:20:27.160 --> 00:20:32.160
Well, we have interest rates falling.

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:35.160
So lower real rates.

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:39.160
And how do consumers respond to that?

00:20:39.160 --> 00:20:42.160
Well, they respond irrationally.

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:52.160
They respond paradoxically via the
mechanism of the economic theory of the
paradox of thrift.

00:20:52.160 --> 00:21:06.160
This occurs by people saving more as
interest rates are falling and consuming
less as well at the same time.

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:16.160
So the paradox is, is that at this part of
the cycle, consumers should be spending
their way out of the contraction.

00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:17.160
But they are not.

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:19.160
They are tightening their belts.

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:25.160
They are hoarding cash, hoarding resources
and consuming less.

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:40.160
So the critics of Keynes' theory of the
paradox of thrift, they criticise it on
the grounds that an increase in savings
from consumers

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:51.160
allows banks to lend more money via an
increase in bank reserves through the
fractional reserve banking system.

00:21:51.160 --> 00:22:05.160
So the idea is that as long as the money
supply is constant, the business cycle
should be self-correcting and shouldn't
require any intervention in the market

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:20.160
because market interest rates should go
down and lead to a natural increase in
lending from the banks and therefore
spending from consumers at some point.

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:28.160
Now, this is all good in theory to say
that the economy will be self-correcting.

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:33.160
If we think about this, then the opposite
must hold true in theory.

00:22:33.160 --> 00:22:45.160
If we have constant money supply growth
when savings rates are lower and
consumption is high during expansionary
periods,

00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:54.160
so in those times of extravagance, then
naturally market interest rates should go
higher or will go higher.

00:22:54.160 --> 00:23:01.160
And then that will naturally lead to a
decrease in lending and therefore consumer
spending.

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:05.160
Unfortunately, guys, the world really
isn't that simple.

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:17.160
It's messy and there's no ultimate truth
or rule book as to what should be
occurring or what happens at any one time
or in any particular business cycle.

00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:21.160
It's obviously can be different every
time.

00:23:21.160 --> 00:23:34.160
But from a trader's and portfolio
manager's perspective, if we're going to
understand consumer sentiment in the US
and use it as a leading indicator,

00:23:34.160 --> 00:23:39.160
we have to understand these dynamics of
consumers.

00:23:39.160 --> 00:23:52.160
We have to understand the business cycle
that is connected, that consumer behavior
is connected to the other parameters of
the economy.

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:58.160
So businesses, for example, and real
interest rates and the money supply.

00:23:58.160 --> 00:24:00.160
We have to understand that.

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:17.160
So whenever we look at the consumer
sentiment index in the United States, we
have to understand the consumer behavior
in the context of all other indicators and
what else is going on in the economy.

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:28.160
We also have to understand that there
could be long periods of irrational
consumer behavior and understand why that
is happening.

00:24:28.160 --> 00:24:32.160
It's probably occurring due to the paradox
of thrift.

00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:38.160
We also have to understand that there
could be very short periods of irrational
behavior.

00:24:38.160 --> 00:24:40.160
Again, paradox of thrift.

00:24:40.160 --> 00:24:50.160
We have to understand that something could
occur that makes consumers begin spending
again in times of economic contraction.

00:24:50.160 --> 00:25:08.160
We also have to understand the dynamic of
the rational behavior that in periods of
expansion that could be extremely long
times of rational behavior from consumers
where they're spending a lot longer than
people expect.

00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:12.160
So we have to take all of this into
context, guys.

00:25:12.160 --> 00:25:16.160
We have to understand consumer behavior
and consumer psychology.

00:25:16.160 --> 00:25:21.160
And we have to understand the limiting
factors on consumers.

00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:39.160
And from a trader's and portfolio
manager's perspective, understand the
other parameters in the economy and how
consumers are linked to the other areas of
the economy and their consumer behavior
and how it's all linked together.

00:25:39.160 --> 00:26:07.160
So this way, in the future, when we pull
up the University of Michigan Consumer
Sentiment Index and we see the number and
we look at the chart and we look at where
we've come from and where we are now and
where we might be going, we get a much
better understanding when we think of the
U.S. consumer and U.S. consumer sentiment
in the context of how consumers behave.

00:26:07.160 --> 00:26:14.160
And how it fits in to the rest of the
economy and the dynamics that are playing
out.

00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:22.160
Because at any one time, things will be
different relative to another time in the
business cycle or in previous business
cycles.

00:26:22.160 --> 00:26:27.160
So we have to understand the context and
keep asking ourselves the questions.

00:26:27.160 --> 00:26:30.160
Why are consumers behaving in this way?

00:26:30.160 --> 00:26:34.160
What does it mean in terms of the business
cycle?

00:26:34.160 --> 00:26:41.160
What does it mean in terms of the context
of real interest rates and the money
supply and money market dynamics?

00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:46.160
What does it mean in the context of
current Fed policy?

00:26:46.160 --> 00:26:56.160
And we're going to get a much clearer
picture if we ask ourselves those
questions at any one moment in time.

00:26:56.160 --> 00:27:12.160
So the U.S. Consumer Sentiment Index
itself, the UMCSI, the University of
Michigan Consumer Sentiment Index, as a
trader or portfolio manager, how do we
actually use it?

00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:22.160
Well, what we do know is that consumer
sentiment historically does have peaks and
troughs, its cycles.

00:27:22.160 --> 00:27:36.160
And when we use it in conjunction with
other macroeconomic indicators, we can
work out at any one given time what is
going on with the U.S. consumer and the
economy.

00:27:36.160 --> 00:27:53.160
And therefore, we have a great indicator
for predicting GDP growth and for calling
the direction of the S&P 500 or the U.S.
stock market and likely stock market
returns and Fed policy.

00:27:53.160 --> 00:28:06.160
And this, like our other leading
indicators that we've looked at so far,
dictates to us the likely directional bias
that we're going to have.

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:13.160
So a long bias, bullish, a short bias,
bearish or a neutral bias.

00:28:13.160 --> 00:28:18.160
So agnostic bias in our portfolios as
traders.

00:28:18.160 --> 00:28:24.160
Now, consumer spending is 67% of the U.S.
economy, as we mentioned previously.

00:28:24.160 --> 00:28:33.160
And it is historically highly predictive
of GDP growth in the future.

00:28:33.160 --> 00:28:43.160
It's also very predictive of stock market
direction and potential stock market
returns and likely Fed policy.

00:28:43.160 --> 00:28:49.160
So guys, let's have a look now at the
detail of the UMCSI.

00:28:49.160 --> 00:28:52.160
Let's have a look at the survey design.

00:28:52.160 --> 00:28:59.160
So the way the survey is put together each
month.

00:28:59.160 --> 00:29:23.160
Basically, the University of Michigan
Consumer Research Center conducts a
telephone survey asking 500, yes, only 500
consumers a series of questions on their
personal finances and their opinions on
business conditions.

00:29:23.160 --> 00:29:36.160
So the way it's broken up, the way it's
split up, it's split into two component
areas that have an equal 50% weighting
given to each area.

00:29:36.160 --> 00:29:43.160
So we have the aggregate consumer
sentiment index, which is the ICS.

00:29:43.160 --> 00:29:48.160
And that is made up of two areas with an
equal 50% weighting.

00:29:48.160 --> 00:29:59.160
That's the index of current conditions,
the ICC, and the index of consumer
expectations, the ICE.

00:29:59.160 --> 00:30:02.160
Now, this is what it looks like on the
surface level.

00:30:02.160 --> 00:30:21.160
But as always, as traders and portfolio
managers, we do have to dig a little
deeper to try and find out which are the
most useful areas for us in predicting
future GDP growth and stock market
direction.

00:30:21.160 --> 00:30:25.160
So let's have a look at how this is broken
down properly.

00:30:25.160 --> 00:30:40.160
We mentioned the two areas, the current
conditions index and the consumer
expectations index, making up the index
overall, the aggregate index of consumer
sentiment.

00:30:40.160 --> 00:30:48.160
And the way that's split up, we have the
five questions and current conditions is
questions number one and five.

00:30:48.160 --> 00:30:51.160
And the expectations index.

00:30:51.160 --> 00:30:58.160
So for consumer expectations, that relates
to questions two, three, and four.

00:30:58.160 --> 00:31:01.160
So let's first have a look at current
conditions.

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:03.160
So one and five.

00:31:03.160 --> 00:31:11.160
Firstly, the 500 consumers are asked, we
are interested in how people are getting
along financially these days.

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:19.160
Would you say that you and your family are
better off or worse off financially than
you were a year ago?

00:31:19.160 --> 00:31:21.160
That's the first question.

00:31:21.160 --> 00:31:23.160
Question number five.

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:31.160
About the big things people buy for their
homes, such as furniture, a refrigerator,
stove, television, and things like that.

00:31:31.160 --> 00:31:40.160
Generally speaking, do you think now is a
good or bad time for people to buy major
household items?

00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:45.160
So these are the two questions that make
up the current conditions index.

00:31:45.160 --> 00:31:51.160
They're basically asking the consumers,
are you better off or worse off
financially than a year ago?

00:31:51.160 --> 00:31:56.160
And are you going to be spending money on
major household items?

00:31:56.160 --> 00:32:09.160
So they are basically being surveyed or
tested, if you like, on their view of
their current financial situation, which
obviously measures current sentiment.

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:11.160
Now let's have a look at expectations.

00:32:11.160 --> 00:32:13.160
So looking into the future.

00:32:13.160 --> 00:32:17.160
So we're looking at questions two, three,
and four.

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:19.160
So question two.

00:32:19.160 --> 00:32:20.160
Looking ahead.

00:32:20.160 --> 00:32:29.160
Do you think that a year from now, you and
your family will be better off financially
or worse off or just about the same as
now?

00:32:29.160 --> 00:32:30.160
Question three.

00:32:30.160 --> 00:32:30.160
Question three.

00:32:30.160 --> 00:32:34.160
Turning to business conditions in the
country as a whole.

00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:41.160
Do you think that during the next 12
months we'll have good times financially
or bad times or what?

00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:43.160
Question four.

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:44.160
Looking ahead.

00:32:44.160 --> 00:32:47.160
Which would you say is more likely?

00:32:47.160 --> 00:32:53.160
That in the country as a whole we'll have
continuous good times during the next five
years or so?

00:32:53.160 --> 00:32:59.160
Or that we will have periods of widespread
unemployment or depression or what?

00:32:59.160 --> 00:33:21.160
So it's pretty obvious here what consumers
are being surveyed on, what they're being
tested on, is their expectations of the
future if they are bullish, bearish or
neutral of their own consumer
expectations, their own consumer sentiment
of the future.

00:33:21.160 --> 00:33:46.160
Now, in terms of the data, you can go to
