for the changes month on month are there
for both the current conditions and the
expectations index as well.

00:35:16.160 --> 00:35:34.160
Now, as we've done in our previous videos
where we've looked at certain times in the
past to look at how certain leading
indicators were behaving at certain
periods of time.

00:35:34.160 --> 00:35:38.160
We can do that obviously as well for the
UMCSI.

00:35:38.160 --> 00:35:47.160
So if we look at the period August 18 to
August 19, that was the period that we've
looked at in previous videos.

00:35:47.160 --> 00:36:01.160
We can see at the headline level of the
UMCSI that we didn't really get a big drop
in consumer sentiment at the aggregate
level during that time.

00:36:01.160 --> 00:36:20.160
So if we look at the data from August 18
to August 19, you can see the move 96.2
down to 89.8, but in July 19, the
aggregate index was 98.4.

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:33.160
So there's only really two moves during
that period at the aggregate level where
the index fell by a decent amount of
points.

00:36:33.160 --> 00:36:38.160
That's January 19 and August 19.

00:36:38.160 --> 00:36:42.160
But we can look at the component parts and
see what that's driven by.

00:36:42.160 --> 00:36:52.160
If we look at the current conditions
index, the current conditions index stays
above 100 the entire time and is extremely
strong.

00:36:52.160 --> 00:36:53.160
Okay.

00:36:53.160 --> 00:36:58.160
But the expectations index is actually
what's driving the aggregate fall.

00:36:58.160 --> 00:37:07.160
So we can see in January 19, a fall down
in expectations.

00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:11.160
That was a move in conjunction with
current conditions.

00:37:11.160 --> 00:37:23.160
But you can see the moves down overall,
and the big move down in August 19 from
expectations of 10.6 points.

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:38.160
So in August 19, the expectations index is
79.9, and the current conditions index is
105.3, leaving the aggregate index at
89.8.

00:37:38.160 --> 00:38:01.160
So what's interesting during this period
is that the aggregate level, so at the
aggregate index, the way we would read
this is that there wasn't a consumer
recession, for want of a better word,
during this period, August 18 to August
19.

00:38:01.160 --> 00:38:08.160
However, there was a very big concern over
consumer expectations.

00:38:08.160 --> 00:38:18.160
There was no current concern, i.e. what
consumers were currently experiencing in
the economy in terms of their sentiment.

00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:22.160
But their expectations of the future were
dwindling.

00:38:22.160 --> 00:38:42.160
And when we put this in a bigger context,
when we think about previous videos in the
video series, and when we looked at this
period, if you recall, we determined that
during this period, it was essentially
down to a manufacturing slowdown, not a
services slowdown.

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:56.160
And if we put this into context now, we
can see that there was no consumer
slowdown at the aggregate level, and the
current conditions of consumer sentiment.

00:38:56.160 --> 00:38:59.160
It was their expectation of the future.

00:38:59.160 --> 00:39:03.160
The consumer was getting tired in the US
in 2019.

00:39:03.160 --> 00:39:14.160
And they were signaling that they were
going to spend less via their own consumer
sentiment and expectations in the future.

00:39:14.160 --> 00:39:24.160
So this is pretty interesting because we
know that the Fed cut rates in July,
September and October.

00:39:24.160 --> 00:39:28.160
So this is how you can see it all starts
to link together.

00:39:28.160 --> 00:39:44.160
And if we look further down in the data,
when we look at the second half of 2019,
we can see the aggregate level by the end
of the year pretty much gets back up to
100.

00:39:44.160 --> 00:39:49.160
In December 2019, we're looking at a
reading of 99.3.

00:39:49.160 --> 00:39:58.160
And you can see a decent recovery in
expectations and definitely a strong
recovery in current conditions.

00:39:58.160 --> 00:40:11.160
If we look further into the COVID era, so
if we look into the first half of 2020, we
can see a total collapse of expectations
in the spring of 2020,

00:40:11.160 --> 00:40:16.160
where we go from above 90 at the beginning
of 2020.

00:40:16.160 --> 00:40:18.160
And we look at the data.

00:40:18.160 --> 00:40:25.160
A few months later, we can see when the
COVID lows go in, which is March 2020.

00:40:25.160 --> 00:40:29.160
We can see expectations of 79.7.

00:40:29.160 --> 00:40:36.160
And we can see in May 2020, expectations
hitting a low of 65.9.

00:40:36.160 --> 00:40:43.160
And the expectations index really
struggled to get moving for the rest of
2020.

00:40:43.160 --> 00:40:56.160
Also, the current conditions index really
struggled as well for most of 2020 after
getting hit due to the COVID economic
shutdown.

00:40:56.160 --> 00:41:06.160
And, you know, just bear in mind, the Fed
actions came in March, April, May, June.

00:41:06.160 --> 00:41:14.160
And the effect that it had on consumers
wasn't actually as big as expected.

00:41:14.160 --> 00:41:19.160
And it took a long time for consumers to
start to come back.

00:41:19.160 --> 00:41:31.160
What you're actually seeing there, when we
look at the 2019 period, and we look at it
compared to the 2020 COVID economic
lockdown period,

00:41:31.160 --> 00:41:41.160
you're looking now at the period of
extended extravagance versus the period of
the paradox of thrift.

00:41:41.160 --> 00:41:47.160
We can also have a look at the global
financial crisis.

00:41:47.160 --> 00:41:51.160
During that time, what was going on with
consumers?

00:41:51.160 --> 00:41:59.160
Well, the UMCSI has peaks and drops like
other leading indicators.

00:41:59.160 --> 00:42:04.160
And we had peak conditions for consumers.

00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:14.160
You'll see that in the data in the
spreadsheet in January 2007, with a
reading at the aggregate level of 96.9.

00:42:14.160 --> 00:42:26.160
And we had a consumer trough in the global
financial crisis of 56.3 in February 2009.

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:39.160
So you can see that's a full two years
from Jan 07, where we had a gradual
decline from peak throughout 2007,

00:42:39.160 --> 00:42:45.160
and then through 2008 to February 2009,
where we had the trough.

00:42:45.160 --> 00:42:48.160
So that's a full two years.

00:42:48.160 --> 00:42:56.160
That's obviously a pretty long period of
time for consumption to be declining.

00:42:56.160 --> 00:43:04.160
So it gives you a good perspective and
understanding of how the consumer behaves
sometimes in the US.

00:43:04.160 --> 00:43:11.160
Coming out of the consumer trough as well,
you can see if we look on the right hand
side,

00:43:11.160 --> 00:43:19.160
we want to know here, how long did it take
for the US consumer to actually recover?

00:43:19.160 --> 00:43:25.160
Well, we know that the Fed was obviously
very, very active during that time.

00:43:25.160 --> 00:43:28.160
They had quantitative easing programmes in
place.

00:43:28.160 --> 00:43:33.160
They also had conventional means.

00:43:33.160 --> 00:43:39.160
So they lowered interest rates extremely
aggressively during the financial crisis.

00:43:39.160 --> 00:43:46.160
But coming out of the financial crisis,
there was non-conventional monetary policy
of quantitative easing.

00:43:46.160 --> 00:43:52.160
So they'd already had one quantitative
easing programme.

00:43:52.160 --> 00:44:01.160
And they implemented the second one, which
didn't actually finish until June 29th,
2012.

00:44:01.160 --> 00:44:11.160
And when we look at the US consumer data,
the Fed's huge, unprecedented actions at
that time,

00:44:11.160 --> 00:44:21.160
it took a long time for those actions to
actually have an effect on the US consumer
and the US consumer recovery.

00:44:21.160 --> 00:44:28.160
The US consumer didn't really recover
until 2012.

00:44:28.160 --> 00:44:40.160
So if you look at the data there, you can
see, for example, the data going back into
the 70s consistently in the first half of
2012.

00:44:40.160 --> 00:44:47.160
But not getting above 80 until the second
half of 2012.

00:44:47.160 --> 00:45:02.160
So as you can see, this is a very long
period of time where the UMCSI at an
aggregate level peaked in January 2007.

00:45:02.160 --> 00:45:11.160
And then even after the massive,
unprecedented Fed policy and actions
during and out of the financial crisis,

00:45:11.160 --> 00:45:19.160
the consumer only really tepidly recovered
by the second half of 2012.

00:45:19.160 --> 00:45:32.160
So five years and the US consumer didn't
even get back to the level where it was in
January 2007,

00:45:32.160 --> 00:45:39.160
even by the second half of 2012, which
when you think about it is obviously a
very long time.

00:45:39.160 --> 00:45:49.160
And what you're experiencing here again is
the, if you like, irrational behavior of
consumers with the paradox of thrift.

00:45:49.160 --> 00:45:58.160
If everybody went out and started spending
their way out of this crisis, it might
have been over a lot quicker.

00:45:58.160 --> 00:46:06.160
And the Fed may have needed to take less
action throughout the following decade.

00:46:06.160 --> 00:46:13.160
But you can see what their behavior was
coming out of the global financial crisis.

00:46:13.160 --> 00:46:20.160
They were obviously tightening their belts
and very, very cautious about their
spending.

00:46:20.160 --> 00:46:28.160
And nowhere near as extravagant with their
spending coming out of the global
financial crisis as they were going into
it.

00:46:28.160 --> 00:46:35.160
So you can see by looking at the data,
these periods of thrift can last a very
long time.

00:46:35.160 --> 00:46:47.160
And also, if you look at periods of
extravagance, periods of expansion, those
periods can last a lot longer than
expected as well.

00:46:47.160 --> 00:46:54.160
But how do we actually use the UMCSI as
traders and portfolio managers?

00:46:54.160 --> 00:47:13.160
Well, unlike our business indicators, the
ISM Manufacturing Index and the Non
Manufacturing Index, the UMCSI doesn't
have a defined level that signifies future
expansion and contraction in the US
economy.

00:47:13.160 --> 00:47:25.160
So you'll notice on our charts, what we've
done is we put a simple trend line average
of the aggregate UMCSI.

00:47:25.160 --> 00:47:30.160
And we correlated this with long-term US
GDP growth.

00:47:30.160 --> 00:47:51.160
So the way we use it as traders and
portfolio managers is we look at the UMCSI
as a kind of zonal guide for general
levels of economic growth and as a
directional guide in conjunction with the
expectations index.

00:47:51.160 --> 00:48:10.160
So we look at the level of the UMCSI as a
guide to basically help us predict the
direction and a certain level of GDP
growth around certain levels or certain
gates.

00:48:10.160 --> 00:48:24.160
So if at any one time we're looking at the
UMCSI and we see multiple readings above
80, well, we know that we have bullish
consumers above this level.

00:48:24.160 --> 00:48:34.160
We should be expecting, based on
historical data, that annualized GDP
growth is going to be 2% or higher.

00:48:34.160 --> 00:48:44.160
If we have readings between 70 and 80, we
kind of have, based on historical
sentiment, benign consumers.

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:54.160
We should expect, when we have readings
between 70 and 80, for annualized GDP
growth to be somewhere between 0% and 2%.

00:48:54.160 --> 00:49:05.160
And if we have readings between 55 and 70,
based on historical data, we have bearish
consumers.

00:49:05.160 --> 00:49:21.160
We should be expecting annualized GDP
growth to be in a very wide range of
negative 5% to plus 1%, but more likely to
be 0 to negative.

00:49:21.160 --> 00:49:30.160
Now, as mentioned previously, the UMCSI,
like other leading indicators, does have
peaks and troughs.

00:49:30.160 --> 00:49:37.160
And essentially, peaks are where the
consumer in the US is at capacity.

00:49:37.160 --> 00:49:44.160
And based on historical probability,
sentiment at some point is more likely to
fall.

00:49:44.160 --> 00:49:57.160
However, when we're looking at it at a
certain period in time, at any one moment
in time, we can't say exactly how long
these conditions will continue.

00:49:57.160 --> 00:50:02.160
Because every cycle is different, because
every cycle is different, and we have to
consider the other factors.

00:50:02.160 --> 00:50:16.160
So we have to consider the other factors
that we looked at earlier, of consumer
behavior and psychology, and also the
other leading indicators, especially the
money market dynamics.

00:50:16.160 --> 00:50:27.160
But the troughs are basically where
consumers in the US have gotten, via the
paradox of thrift, too bearish and
miserable.

00:50:27.160 --> 00:50:39.160
And they now have capacity for spending,
so they can go out and begin buying
discretionary items instead of just
necessities.

00:50:39.160 --> 00:50:54.160
But again, like the peaks, every cycle is
different, and we can't say exactly how
long this period of bearish consumer
sentiment or thriftiness is going to last.

00:50:54.160 --> 00:51:06.160
And again, we have to use it in
conjunction with everything else that's
going on in the other leading indicators
at any one moment in time.

00:51:06.160 --> 00:51:17.160
But let's have a look at the chart of the
data where we compare the UM-CSI aggregate
versus real GDP growth.

00:51:17.160 --> 00:51:23.160
So we have the UM-CSI on the right-hand
side, and we have real GDP growth on the
left-hand side.

00:51:23.160 --> 00:51:28.160
The blue line on the chart is the UM-CSI
reading.

00:51:28.160 --> 00:51:39.160
The green line, the green lines are the
bars for the level of GDP growth that's
reported quarterly.

00:51:39.160 --> 00:51:51.160
And the red line is a long-term average
growth rate of US annualized GDP.

00:51:51.160 --> 00:52:09.160
So you can see when we look at moments in
history where we've had multiple readings
above the 80 level, this is generally
correlated with GDP growth of 2% or above.

00:52:09.160 --> 00:52:19.160
And then when we have multiple readings
between 70 and 80, we're looking at benign
consumers.

00:52:19.160 --> 00:52:27.160
And this generally correlates anything
between 0% and 2%.

00:52:27.160 --> 00:52:37.160
And then if we have multiple readings
between 55 and 70, we tend to get growth
between minus 5% and 1%.

00:52:37.160 --> 00:52:54.160
So this is looking back across the whole
data set and looking at a zonal approach
to the UM-CSI versus predicting direction
and size of GDP growth.

00:52:54.160 --> 00:53:09.160
Now, something we've also done in the
downloads area is look at the UM-CSI
versus S&P 500 direction and year-on-year
percentage returns.

00:53:09.160 --> 00:53:38.160
And what you can see by looking at the
