01:06:30.160 --> 01:06:45.160
Now there are other things that when we're
looking at the US consumer, we have to be
aware of in terms of things in the bigger
picture just to fill everything in.

01:06:45.160 --> 01:06:52.160
At any one moment in time, it's just good
to be aware of these things that we're
going to look at now.

01:06:52.160 --> 01:07:01.160
Because it tells us for consumers in the
United States, the capacity of the
consumer.

01:07:01.160 --> 01:07:11.160
So if you recall from the causality of
consumer behavior and spending patterns
from earlier slides,

01:07:11.160 --> 01:07:20.160
we looked at consumer spending and savings
rates being a function of real interest
rates and the money supply,

01:07:20.160 --> 01:07:26.160
which are obviously both manipulated and
managed by the Federal Reserve.

01:07:26.160 --> 01:07:36.160
the Fed is obviously very active in
managing real interest rates and the money
supply.

01:07:36.160 --> 01:07:44.160
And this affects the way consumers behave,
the way they spend and the way they save.

01:07:44.160 --> 01:07:59.160
And there's certain statistics we can look
at in the US economy to get an
appreciation and understanding in the
bigger picture of consumer capacity.

01:07:59.160 --> 01:08:13.160
So something we would look at on a regular
basis every quarter to understand the
consumer capacity is US household debt to
GDP.

01:08:13.160 --> 01:08:22.160
So the level of household debt to gross
domestic product expressed in percentage
terms.

01:08:22.160 --> 01:08:23.160
So what is household debt?

01:08:23.160 --> 01:08:30.160
Well, it's made up of mortgages, consumer
loans, credit card balances and auto
finance.

01:08:30.160 --> 01:08:40.160
And if you look at the chart on the left
hand side, our data from trading economics
is going back to the 1950s.

01:08:40.160 --> 01:08:52.160
And you can obviously see a steady rise in
the level of household debt as a
percentage of GDP all the way up to
present day.

01:08:52.160 --> 01:09:01.160
And obviously it peaked around the mid
2000s before the financial crisis.

01:09:01.160 --> 01:09:13.160
And where we are now at the time of
recording is essentially, if you look at
the right hand side for a zoomed in
picture, 76% of GDP.

01:09:13.160 --> 01:09:20.160
So household debt is 76% of GDP in the
United States at the time of recording.

01:09:21.160 --> 01:09:23.160
So why would we look at this on a regular
basis?

01:09:23.160 --> 01:09:36.160
Well, we just want to get an appreciation
and understanding as to whether there's
actually capacity for consumers to grow or
be bullish in the future.

01:09:38.160 --> 01:09:50.160
So being at 76% and coming down from a
level of almost close to 100% versus GDP,
there's obviously slack and capacity in
the economy.

01:09:50.160 --> 01:09:53.160
There's no capacity for this to occur.

01:09:53.160 --> 01:10:02.160
So there's enough capacity for an
increased debt load relative to GDP to
occur.

01:10:02.160 --> 01:10:12.160
But obviously, a lot depends on interest
rates and the cost of servicing any
outstanding debt in the economy.

01:10:12.160 --> 01:10:27.160
Something else we would look at on a
regular basis to understand capacity
issues is US household debt service
payments as a percentage of disposable
personal income.

01:10:27.160 --> 01:10:46.160
And if you look at the chart coming from
1980 up until time of recording, you can
see obviously the percentage of US
household debt service payments versus
disposable personal income.

01:10:46.160 --> 01:10:49.160
It's been up and down for that entire
period.

01:10:49.160 --> 01:10:58.160
And typically before 2010, it was between
10% and 13%.

01:10:58.160 --> 01:11:18.160
But you can see given the moves that we've
had in interest rates over the last decade
from the time of recording over the last
decade, you can see that this percentage
of household debt service payments
relative to personal income has been
dropping by quite a lot.

01:11:18.160 --> 01:11:19.160
So what does that mean?

01:11:19.160 --> 01:11:25.160
Well, servicing the debt relative to
income has become a lot cheaper for
households in the United States.

01:11:25.160 --> 01:11:36.160
So it means that there's obviously
capacity to occur in terms of servicing
debt and the total debt load.

01:11:36.160 --> 01:11:43.160
So that gives consumers room to be
bullish.

01:11:43.160 --> 01:11:56.160
Something else we would look at on a
quarterly basis is a household and
non-profit organization, one to four
family residential mortgage liability.

01:11:56.160 --> 01:12:01.160
And we look at the total debt load here on
those mortgages.

01:12:01.160 --> 01:12:12.160
And if you look at the left hand side, in
billions of dollars, you can see it's
above the 10,000 billion mark, which is 10
trillion.

01:12:12.160 --> 01:12:15.160
Does that mean there's no capacity left?

01:12:15.160 --> 01:12:16.160
Of course not.

01:12:16.160 --> 01:12:19.160
This is just a dollar figure.

01:12:19.160 --> 01:12:22.160
So of course, more capacity can occur.

01:12:22.160 --> 01:12:29.160
But you can see the general trend since
the 70s has been on a big rise.

01:12:29.160 --> 01:12:32.160
We peaked in the financial crisis.

01:12:32.160 --> 01:12:45.160
And it looks like the one to four family
residential mortgage liability is growing
above the global financial crisis level.

01:12:45.160 --> 01:12:52.160
So again, does this mean there's a
capacity issue if this thing goes up a lot
higher?

01:12:52.160 --> 01:13:03.160
Not necessarily, as mentioned before on
the previous slide, it's the level of
interest that is important here as well.

01:13:03.160 --> 01:13:15.160
So it's the size of the debt load and the
cost that households incur relative to
their personal income in servicing that
debt load.

01:13:15.160 --> 01:13:31.160
So we keep an eye on these things on a
quarterly basis and we just take note of
them and what it might mean for consumer
capacity in terms of household debt and
the cost of servicing that debt.

01:13:31.160 --> 01:13:44.160
And we put it into context with our other
leading indicators and we fill in the
bigger picture and try to understand at
any one moment in time what's going on in
the economy.

01:13:44.160 --> 01:13:59.160
And of course, if you look at the chart
here and compare it to the household debt
charts on the previous slides and what
have we got here, we've got the 10 year
yield adjusted for CPI.

01:13:59.160 --> 01:14:01.160
So adjusted for inflation.

01:14:01.160 --> 01:14:06.160
So we've got the real 10 year yield since
1980.

01:14:06.160 --> 01:14:26.160
And this obviously, if you look at the
modern context, gives you an idea of Fed
policy and what they're trying to achieve
here and think about it in the context of
the previous slides and household debt.

01:14:26.160 --> 01:14:40.160
So real interest rates have been coming
down over the last 40 years and coming
down from a level of around 8% to modern
day as negative.

01:14:40.160 --> 01:14:46.160
Yes, of course, there's a big question
here in that over the bigger picture.

01:14:46.160 --> 01:15:03.160
Is it necessary for this to happen because
the debt load is so high that the Fed has
to be in the market creating conditions of
zero to negative real rates in order for
households to service that debt.

01:15:03.160 --> 01:15:13.160
And the big question is their capacity for
further debt to be added to the US
economy.

01:15:13.160 --> 01:15:26.160
Well, we looked at the previous slides and
it can be argued both ways, but we just
need to keep an eye on these numbers on a
quarterly basis.

01:15:26.160 --> 01:15:33.160
So the household debt numbers and, of
course, real rates, we need to be keeping
an eye on them all the time.

01:15:33.160 --> 01:15:50.160
And thinking about what that means in the
context of consumer capacity, consumer
behavior and consumer psychology and also
what the UMCSI is telling us.

01:15:50.160 --> 01:15:57.160
So let's have a summary of the University
of Michigan Consumer Sentiment Index.

01:15:57.160 --> 01:16:01.160
So we know that consumer spending is
dominant.

01:16:01.160 --> 01:16:07.160
It makes up 67% of all spending in the
United States.

01:16:07.160 --> 01:16:14.160
So it's obviously very important for us to
be on top of this as traders and portfolio
managers.

01:16:14.160 --> 01:16:26.160
How consumers feel about their current, so
coincident, and expected future income
affects GDP growth, which is lagging.

01:16:26.160 --> 01:16:29.160
So it is a leading indicator.

01:16:29.160 --> 01:16:41.160
And not just that, the UMCSI is a powerful
leading indicator of US GDP growth and S&P
500 direction and potential returns.

01:16:41.160 --> 01:16:49.160
So it's vitally important to us as
professional traders, our portfolio
managers for our portfolio buyers.

01:16:49.160 --> 01:17:08.160
Because consumer spending makes up such a
large part of all spending in the US, the
US consumer drives US GDP growth and
global GDP growth and stock market returns
in the US and the rest of the world.

01:17:08.160 --> 01:17:11.160
How do we use it as professional traders?

01:17:11.160 --> 01:17:28.160
Well, we use it as a guide in conjunction
with money market indicators and our other
leading indicators to predict trends and
turning points in the business cycle and
therefore getting our portfolio
directional bias.

01:17:28.160 --> 01:17:33.160
So net long or short or even neutral
portfolio bias.

01:17:33.160 --> 01:17:53.160
And remember, there is no defined levels
like the business activity indices, like
the ISM manufacturing and
non-manufacturing indices, where we have
the 50 level signifying above 50 expansion
and below 50 contraction.

01:17:53.160 --> 01:17:56.160
The UMCSI doesn't have that.

01:17:56.160 --> 01:18:10.160
We have to use it as a zonal guide and
correlate it with GDP growth in those
zones and stock market direction and
returns to get a rough guide of what to
expect.

01:18:10.160 --> 01:18:22.160
And we also have to be very careful in our
understanding of consumer behavior and
psychology when looking at consumer
sentiment.

01:18:22.160 --> 01:18:35.160
So, periods of thrift, because of the
paradox of thrift, can occur in small
windows of time, but they can also occur
in very long windows of time as well.

01:18:35.160 --> 01:18:38.160
And we shouldn't be surprised when that
happens.

01:18:38.160 --> 01:18:47.160
Additionally, on the flip side, periods of
extravagance can occur for a much longer
period of time as well.

01:18:47.160 --> 01:19:00.160
So, we have to be aware of that when we're
thinking about peaks and troughs and also
the effect the UMCSI has on GDP growth
going forward.

01:19:00.160 --> 01:19:16.160
But because, as well, the UMCSI in
measuring consumer sentiment and because
it's so important in terms of consumer
spending driving the economy, of course,
the Fed is looking at it as well.

01:19:16.160 --> 01:19:24.160
So, in the back of our minds always, at
any one point in time, when we're asking
ourselves, what does all this mean?

01:19:24.160 --> 01:19:35.160
We have to ask ourselves the question as
well, is the Fed looking at this and is
the Fed likely to engage in some sort of
policy response?

01:19:35.160 --> 01:19:55.160
So, by using the UMCSI in conjunction with
our money market leading indicators and
our other macroeconomic indicators, we're
going to have a very good understanding of
the US economy, money flows and potential
Fed policy.

